/*
Bitwise facts you rely on

a ^ a = 0

a ^ 0 = a

XOR is commutative and associative

Time complexity

O(n)

Space complexity

O(1)

Dry run example
Input
nums = [4, 1, 2, 1, 2]

Step-by-step
Step	i	val before	val after (val ^ i)
1	4	0	4
2	1	4	5
3	2	5	7
4	1	7	6
5	2	6	4
Final result
4


All duplicates canceled each other.

Simple intuition

XOR of a number with itself is 0

XOR of a number with 0 is the number

Order doesn’t matter (commutative & associative)

All paired numbers cancel out

The number that appears once remains
*/
class Solution {
    public int singleNumber(int[] nums) {
        int val = 0;
        for(int i:nums){
            val ^= i;
        }
        return val;
    }
}
